 @SwirlingDragonMist  Nothing prevents you from cramming your GPU into a separate enclosure and connecting it to a 20" custom aftermarket cooler as-is.
The issue with wanting something like that straight out of the box is the fact that it's going to be more expensive, and it's going to leave you with more e-waste once it's obsoleted 6 months down the line.

What we need right now is more power efficiency. The top-of-the-line graphics cards are already as powerful as any gamer could really ever want. Only a millionaire would try to game at 8K, and the current top-of-the-line handles 4K just fine with DLSS/FSR enabled.

 @mediocremaker3867  Yeah, many homes are older with older circuits that they've used for years. 

I'm not as interested in sustained loads but rather if and when a power hungry GPU spikes for milli-seconds to seconds if that would trigger a standard circuit. 

It would be interesting to see how the computer's power supply can handle it as well. Especially with the next gen cards going for around 600 watt loads.

 @chessmyantidrug  There is no justifying 600 watts power when the opposing brand is vastly superior in terms of efficiency. 

It's like saying "Hey, my Tesla beats your GT Ford 2005 not only in a drag race but in terms of 550km range whereas the GT Food 2005 has 550 horsepower only has a 400km range with higher power consumption". 

NVIDIA cards are like that, pump more power at the problem. The GTX or RTX 4000 series is going to be one of the most power inefficient cards ever to be produced. And all for what? An extra 5-10fps increase?

